// Validate and clean email for display purposes
static cleanEmailForDisplay(email: string): string {
  return email.replace(/\+[a-zA-Z0-9]{25}/, "");
}

async getOutputRecurringSeatedBookings(bookingsIds: number[], showAttendees: boolean) {
  const databaseBookings = await this.bookingsRepository.getByIdsWithAttendeesWithBookingSeatAndUserAndEvent(bookingsIds);

  const bookingsMap = new Map(databaseBookings.map(booking => [booking.id, booking]));

  const transformed = bookingsIds.map(bookingId => {
    const databaseBooking = bookingsMap.get(bookingId);
    if (!databaseBooking) {
      throw new Error(`Booking with id=${bookingId} was not found in the database`);
    }
    return this.getOutputRecurringSeatedBooking(databaseBooking, showAttendees);
  });

  return transformed;
}

getHost(user: DatabaseUser | null) {
  if (!user) {
    return {
      id: "unknown",
      name: "unknown",
      email: "unknown",
      displayEmail: this.getDisplayEmail("unknown"),
      username: "unknown",
    };
  }

findings.body:
`getDisplayEmail` uses `/\+[a-zA-Z0-9]{25}/` with no end anchor. It strips the first `+` followed by exactly 25 alphanumerics, wherever that happens. If the tag is longer than 25 characters, the leftover characters stay in the local part. I checked this directly: `bob+cmidditrv0000mza4q93hbcau1@example.com` becomes `bob1@example.com`, which is a different, wrong address. The helper is also applied to every email and guest in the response, so this affects any user whose plus-tag is 26+ characters, not just managed users.

Anchoring the match to the `@` that ends the local part avoids this. Strings that don't end with a 25-character tag then pass through unchanged.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/ee/bookings/2024-08-13/services/output.service.ts" lines="96-98"}

::suggestion{file="apps/api/v2/src/ee/bookings/2024-08-13/services/output.service.ts" lines="96-98"}
private getDisplayEmail(email: string): string {
  return email.replace(/\+[a-zA-Z0-9]{25}(?=@)/, "");
}

The PR description says the pattern is 'consistent with google-calendar.service.ts', but the matching code is in `packages/app-store/googlecalendar/lib/CalendarAuth.ts`, which uses the same unanchored regex. Fix both, or keep this one unanchored only on purpose and document why.

`BookingAttendee.cleanEmailForDisplay` is added to the output DTO but nothing calls it. The service has its own `getDisplayEmail` with the same regex. That leaves two copies of the stripping rule that can drift apart, and a reviewer reading the DTO would reasonably assume it is the one in use.

Keep one implementation. If the DTO needs it, export a shared helper and call it from the service. Otherwise delete the static method.

::diff{file="packages/platform/types/bookings/2024-08-13/outputs/booking.output.ts" lines="58-63"}

In `getOutputBooking`'s `bookingFieldsResponses.guests` handling, `getDisplayEmail` is passed as `this.getDisplayEmail` to `.map(...)`. The helper doesn't use `this`, so this works today. But it is an unbound instance method, and the next person who adds a `this` reference will get `undefined` at runtime with no compile-time warning. The sibling code in `getOutputRecurringBooking` uses an arrow function (`(guest: string) => this.getDisplayEmail(guest)`), so the two places are also inconsistent.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/ee/bookings/2024-08-13/services/output.service.ts" lines="166-173"}

Use the arrow form in both places.

The PR description says the OpenAPI spec was auto-updated, but the diff shows hand edits. Four `required` arrays were changed to `"required": [` with no indentation, while the surrounding JSON is indented. The file still parses, so tooling works, but the next generator run will produce a noisy diff, and the hand-edit contradicts the auto-update claim.

Regenerate the spec with the project's generator rather than editing it by hand, so the indentation and ordering match the rest of the file.

::diff{file="docs/api-reference/v2/openapi.json" lines="31729-31737"}

Six new `oasdiff` breaking-change entries are added to `.github/oasdiff-err-ignore.txt`. They cover `oneOf` schema changes on booking endpoints (POST /v2/bookings, GET /v2/bookings/{uid}, and the cancel, confirm, decline, and guests endpoints). The additions are plausibly benign, but nothing in the PR explains why each one is acceptable. Future regressions that match these patterns will also be hidden. The file also ends without a trailing newline.

A short comment per entry, or a note in the PR, would make it reviewable. A narrower ignore pattern would be better still.

